Using a non-null assertion (`!`) here instead of a descriptive guard. Although `args.project` is checked two lines above, the project rule says required lookups should throw a descriptive `Error` rather than asserting. Change to:
```typescript
const project = PROJECTS.find((p) => p.name === args.project);
if (!project) throw new Error(`Unknown project: '${args.project}'. Run --list-projects for options.`);
```

Both branches of this ternary produce identical runtime values — they only differ in TypeScript's inferred type. The intent (narrowing `args` so `model` and `effort` get their discriminated-union types) is valid but the duplication is misleading. Add a short comment, or if TypeScript is happy with it, just write the assignment directly without the ternary:
```typescript
// Ternary is for TS narrowing; both branches are the same at runtime
const variant: AgentVariant = args.agent === 'claude'
  ? { agent: args.agent, model: args.model, effort: args.effort }
  : { agent: args.agent, model: args.model, effort: args.effort };
```
Alternatively verify whether a direct assignment satisfies the discriminated-union type and remove the ternary entirely.

No validation that `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is present before running. If the env var is absent the SDK throws a confusing deep error. Per project rules, required env vars in test/eval scripts should be explicitly checked and fail fast with a descriptive message:
```typescript
const apiKey = process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY;
if (!apiKey) throw new Error('ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is required to run the Claude agent. Set it in your environment.');
```
Add this guard near the top of `execute()` (or at module load time) before calling `query()`.

`Math.round(s % 60)` can produce `60` when the fractional seconds component is ≥ 59.5 (e.g., `s = 119.6` → `Math.floor(119.6 / 60) = 1`, `Math.round(59.6) = 60` → `"1m60s"` instead of `"2m0s"`). Fix:
```typescript
export const formatDuration = (s: number) => {
  const totalSecs = Math.round(s);
  return totalSecs < 60 ? `${totalSecs}s` : `${Math.floor(totalSecs / 60)}m${totalSecs % 60}s`;
};
```
The `utils.test.ts` tests don't cover this edge case.
